{
 "about": "How the FiSH switch of this case was built, and the measured numbers behind article sections 6 to 9. The switch was written by experiment scripts, not by a gne command, and its weights (keys, prediction rows, LoRA files) are not published. All numbers here are copied from the measurements (they need the model to recompute).",
 "form": [
  "The CHiPS heads are left unchanged and CHiPS is not applied. In each layer L that holds a chosen head, two MLP neurons are overwritten:",
  "W_gate rows <- the key (least squares on the build replies, equal weight per cell of scene and stretch of the reply; open = serious questions, closed = light and unrelated questions; the key is read with no edit applied)",
  "W_up rows (both neurons) <- a ridge-regression vector that predicts T_L = sum_i s_i (r_L . v_i) from the MLP input h, where i runs over the chosen heads of layer L, s_i is the head's strength and v_i its output",
  "W_down columns <- -r_L / 6.4 for neuron 1 and +r_L / 6.4 for neuron 2",
  "added vector = -g x (predicted T_L) x r_L, about -g x T_L x r_L: the change CHiPS would make in that layer when g is near 1, nothing when g is near 0",
  "g is the pair's switch value: about 0 when the key output rho is below 0.4 and about 1 above 0.8 (constants 16, 0.4, 0.8 and 6.4, as in the explainer of FiSH)",
  "the key opens on every position of the prompt and the reply of a serious question; the effect was measured to come mostly while the reply is written",
  "the control key is built by the same procedure with the scene labels of the build questions shuffled"
 ],
 "neuron_pairs_per_layer": 1,
 "constants": {
  "gate_scale": 16,
  "rho_low": 0.4,
  "rho_high": 0.8,
  "pair_gain": 6.4
 },
